---
/**
 * Privacy policy: no analytics, no cookies until you talk with Emily, then one; what the conversation collects,
 * who handles it and for how long. Every particular comes from data/legal.ts. The page changes before the site
 * changes what it collects.
 */
import { legal } from '../data/legal';
import { site } from '../data/site.ts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const updated = '16 October 2026';
const browserKeys = [
  { key: 'em-theme', where: 'local storage', what: 'whether you chose the dark or the light theme.' },
  {
    key: 'em-tour',
    where: 'local storage',
    what: 'how far you got on the guided tour, so you can pick it up where you left off.',
  },
  {
    key: 'em-colo',
    where: 'session storage',
    what: 'the three-letter code of the Cloudflare data centre that served you, kept for the readouts on the development pages. It is forgotten when you close the tab.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="How emersa.io handles your data: no analytics, no cookies until you talk with Emily, and what that conversation keeps."
>
  <article class="container container-narrow section">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Legal</p>
      <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        This site runs no analytics. We receive personal data only when you email us, or when you choose to talk
        with Emily.
      </p>
      <p class="page-meta">Last updated {updated}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="who">Who we are</h2>
      <p>
        {legal.company}, trading as {legal.tradingName}, is the controller of any personal data this site handles.
        Company No. {legal.companyNo}, registered in {legal.registeredIn}. {legal.registeredOffice}.
      </p>
      <p>
        Questions about this policy go to <a href={`mailto:${legal.privacyEmail}`}>{legal.privacyEmail}</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="summary">The short version</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No cookies are set unless you choose to talk with Emily; then one, described below.</li>
        <li>No analytics, no advertising and no tracking pixels. The only third-party code that can load is the
          person check and the voice service, and only once you tap Start talking.</li>
        <li>An email you send us and, if you choose, a conversation with Emily are the only times this site handles
          personal data.</li>
        <li>We never store your IP address.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="browser">What stays in your browser</h2>
      <p>
        The site keeps a few small things in your browser's own storage. They never leave your device and we cannot
        read them:
      </p>
      <ul>
        {
          browserKeys.map((item) => (
            <li>
              <code>{item.key}</code> ({item.where}): {item.what}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p>Clearing your browser's site data for emersa.io removes them.</p>

      <h2 id="email">When you email us</h2>
      <p>
        Writing to {legal.contactEmail} goes to our own mailbox. We use what you send to answer you and for nothing
        else, keep it for as long as the conversation needs, and delete it when you ask us to.
      </p>

      <h2 id="hosting">Hosting and logs</h2>
      <p>
        Cloudflare serves every page from its network. To do that it processes your IP address for the duration of the
        request, as any host must, under its own security measures. We keep no visitor logs ourselves: our own metrics
        count requests by route and outcome only, never by person.
      </p>

      <h2 id="voice">Talking with Emily</h2>
      <p>
        On the home page you can talk with Emily. You type by default; your microphone is used only while you hold
        the microphone button, or hold Emily herself, and it closes the moment you let go. Nothing starts until you
        read what happens and tap Start talking.
      </p>
      <h3>What is collected</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Your voice</strong>, only while you hold the microphone button or Emily. It is turned into words to answer you
          and is not kept as a recording by us.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What you say and what Emily replies</strong>, as text, so she can pick the conversation up next
          time.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A few facts</strong> picked out of the conversation, such as your name, your company or what you
          are interested in.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Files you add.</strong> We keep only the text read from the file (at most 20,000 characters), never
          the file itself; the first 6,000 characters are given to Emily for the conversation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>One cookie</strong>, <code>em_vid</code>: a random identifier, set only after you tap Start talking,
          readable only by our server, so Emily knows it is you next time. It is not used for anything else and is
          not shared.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Who handles it</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Cloudflare, Inc.</strong> runs the site and the database that holds the memory. When the person
          check (Turnstile) is enabled, it also checks that you are a person before a conversation starts.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Stripe</strong> takes the payment when you buy minutes, on its own page. It receives your card
          details and email address and is responsible for them under its own privacy policy. We receive a record of
          each payment (Stripe's reference, the amount, the currency and the browser it was for) and keep it for six
          years, as UK accounting rules require; Forget me deletes your minutes but not that record.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Convai Technologies Inc.</strong>, in the United States, reads what you type or say, writes
          Emily's replies and speaks them. The transfer is covered by the safeguards UK law requires.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Microsoft</strong> handles speech only if we switch to its Azure speech service. We will update
          this list before that happens.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Why, and for how long</h3>
      <p>
        We rely on your consent, given when you tap Start talking. Memory is kept for 12 months after your last
        conversation and then deleted automatically. Talking is free: three conversations on your first day, then one
        a day, each up to five minutes.
      </p>
      <p>
        To keep the free conversations fair, we also count them per network for the day. Your IP address is not
        stored for this: we keep only a one-way code made from it (a keyed hash), which cannot be turned back into the
        address, and the count is deleted after two days. Free conversations are not offered through a
        VPN, proxy or Tor; we tell those apart from the network's owner name that Cloudflare reports, which we do not
        keep. This rests on our legitimate interest in preventing abuse of a free service.
      </p>
      <h3>Seeing and erasing it</h3>
      <p>
        In the Talk panel, What Emily remembers (under More) shows everything held about you. Forget me deletes all
        of it at once, including the copy at Convai, and removes the cookie.
      </p>
      <p>Emily is an AI and may make mistakes.</p>

      <h2 id="tools">The QR code generator</h2>
      <p>
        The QR code generator runs entirely in your browser. Nothing you type into it is sent anywhere, counted or
        kept; close the tab and it is gone.
      </p>

      <h2 id="rights">Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Under the UK General Data Protection Regulation you can ask us for a copy of the personal data we hold about
        you, ask us to correct or delete it, restrict or object to how we use it, and ask for it in a portable form.
        Write to <a href={`mailto:${legal.privacyEmail}`}>{legal.privacyEmail}</a> and we will reply within one month.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are not happy with how we handle your data you can complain to the Information Commissioner's Office at{' '}
        <a href="https://ico.org.uk/">ico.org.uk</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="children">Children</h2>
      <p>This site is not aimed at children and we do not knowingly collect personal data from them.</p>

      <h2 id="changes">Changes</h2>
      <p>
        We update this page when the site changes what it collects, and the date at the top moves with it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="page-tour">
      <a href="/?tour=technology">{site.tour.resumeHere}</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
